BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261114T061612Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54099767102537
DTSTART:20261029T190000Z
DTEND:20261029T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join artist Maya Freelon for an immersive gallery tour of Groun
 ded\, exploring the evolution of her work\, creative practice\, and the Fr
 eelon lineage and legacy. Through conversation\, "slow looking"\, gentle m
 ovement\, awareness\, and discovery\, participants will engage with the te
 xtures\, colors\, materials\, and memories embedded in Freelon’s work.\n
 \nThe tour will include artwork by Maya Freelon\, alongside photographs by
  her late father\, Phil Freelon — NC State Design School alum and archit
 ect of the renovated Gregg Museum of Art & Design — and her great-grandf
 ather\, Allan Freelon\, Sr.\, a pioneer African American Impressionist pai
 nter active during the Harlem Renaissance.\n\nThe exhibition space welcome
 s the practice of grounding\, as Maya guides visitors through mindful mome
 nts that evoke the senses and invite reflection on ancestry\, healing\, jo
 y\, resilience\, and connection. Participants will leave with grounding pr
 actices they can carry beyond the museum\, using mindfulness as a tool for
  Grounding wherever they go.\n\nThis event is free but registration is req
 uired.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Guided Tour and Meditation with Maya Freelon
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/guided-tour-and-meditation-wi
 th-maya-freelon
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Training
CATEGORIES:Community Events
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
CATEGORIES:Student Life
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC State Gregg Museum of Art & Design
DTSTAMP:20261114T061612Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_54100007281045
DTSTART:20261107T180000Z
DTEND:20261107T220000Z
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a fun-filled afternoon at the Gregg Museum of Art &
  Design to celebrate the opening of Grounded by acclaimed artist Maya Free
 lon. \n\nDrawing on her signature use of delicate tissue paper\, Freelon t
 ransforms the galleries into an immersive environment that honors both fra
 gility and resilience. In Grounded\, the red clay and soil of North Caroli
 na become central materials and metaphors\, asking us to consider what it 
 means to create sanctuary in uncertain times and how the land itself holds
  memory\, pain\, and possibility.\n\nThe community celebration will includ
 e local organizations highlighting African American heritage and wellness\
 , along with special performances by Tre. Charles\, Johnny Lee Chapman III
 \, and NC State’s Panoramic Dance Project. \n\nYou are invited to Connec
 t. Celebrate. Get Grounded. Please read more about the timed activities be
 low.\n\nCelebration Program Schedule\n\n1 p.m. | Celebration Begins\nOngoi
 ng Activities 1–5 p.m.\n\nDiscover everything happening across the lawn\
 , garden\, and museum! Create your own contribution to our Community Tissu
 e Paper Quilt in the Grounded exhibition\, meet local partners\, and enjoy
  food from the Empanadas and More Food Truck. Complimentary non-alcoholic 
 drinks are available while supplies last\, plus FREE Howling Cow ice cream
  for our first 200 visitors!\n\n1:15–1:45 p.m. | Panoramic Dance Perform
 ance\nAdams Gallery\nEnjoy a performance inspired by Grounded choreographe
 d by Christa Oliver and performed by NC State students in the Department o
 f Performing Arts & Technology and featuring Atiba Rorie and Khalid Saleem
 . Standing room viewing only\; seating available upon accommodation reques
 t.\n\n1:50-2:10 p.m. | Opening Remarks\nMain Lobby\nHear remarks from arti
 st-in-residence Maya Freelon\, Museum Director Sara Segerlin and special g
 uests. Standing room viewing only\; seating available upon accommodation r
 equest.\n\n2:15–3:45 p.m. | Tre. Charles & Band\nMuseum Lawn\nBring a la
 wn chair or a blanket and sit on the lawn to enjoy live music by Durham-ba
 sed artist Tre. Charles and his guest band. \n\n2:45–3 p.m. and 3:30–3
 :45 p.m. | Spoken Word with Johnny Lee Chapman III\nBlack-Sanderson Galler
 y\nMeet Raleigh Poet Laureate Johnny Lee Chapman III in the Crafting Sanct
 uaries exhibition. Limited seating\; extra seating available upon accommod
 ation request. \n\n3:45–4:15 p.m. | Panoramic Dance Performance\nAdams G
 alleryand Woodson Galleries\nEnjoy a second performance by Christa Oliver 
 and NC State students (details above)..\n\n4–5 p.m. | Ask Me! Guide\nHis
 toric Chancellor’s Residence\nMeet Khadija McNair\, manager of NC Freedo
 m Park\, near the fireplace and ask about Community Stories: Sustaining No
 rth Carolina Black Heritage.\n\n5 p.m. | Celebration Concludes \n\nParking
 : The Gregg Museum parking lot will be closed for the celebration. Visitor
 s may park in the Ferndell public parking lot or at Pullen Arts Center\, w
 here parking is free on Saturdays. These alternate parking locations are a
 pproximately 2–4 blocks from the museum. Please visit the Gregg Museum w
 ebsite for additional parking information: https://gregg.arts.ncsu.edu/abo
 ut/visit/alternate-parking-locations-for-the-gregg-museum/\n\nFree and ope
 n to the public. Please register for the general event\, and feel free to 
 drop in anytime between 1 and 5 p.m.
GEO:35.784762;-78.661636
LOCATION:Gregg Museum of Art & Design
SUMMARY:Opening Celebration of Grounded by Maya Freelon
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.ncsu.edu/event/opening-celebration-of-ground
 ed-by-maya-freelon
CATEGORIES:Community Events
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
